# Slack access via Incoming Webhooks (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-08-09): **Agents are not blocked from Slack.**
Cursor has Incoming Webhooks for Phil and Robert. Missing Slack MCP is **not**
a reason to skip notifications or ask the operator to paste a draft.

## The rule

> **When an agent must notify Phil and/or Robert on Slack (major complete,
> partner share, status that previously said “Slack blocked / no MCP”),
> send via the lab Incoming Webhooks. Do not claim Slack is unavailable
> because `plugin-slack-slack` / Slack MCP is absent. Prefer Slack MCP when
> it is connected; otherwise use the webhook path below. Never commit
> webhook URLs into git.**

## Recipients

| Alias | Who | When to use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Phil** | Operator | Major completes, blockers needing operator decision, lab status |
| **Robert** | Partner | Partner-facing drops, MSI/share notes, joint feature summaries |

Default for “notify the team” / “Slack Phil + Robert”: send **both**.

## Where the URLs live (secrets — not in this rule)

Canonical local store (machine-only, gitignored):

`c:\analog-pim\.cursor\secrets\slack-webhooks.json`

Shape:

```json
{
  "phil": "https://hooks.slack.com/services/…",
  "robert": "https://hooks.slack.com/services/…"
}
```

If that file is missing: report **BLOCKED — Slack webhooks file missing** once,
and leave a draft under `docs/dev/` or the product `docs/` path. Do **not**
invent that Slack itself is unsupported.

## How to send (required pattern)

Prefer the helper (reads the secrets file, never echoes the URL):

```powershell
powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File `
  c:\analog-pim\.cursor\automations\Send-AgentSlack.ps1 `
  -To Both `
  -Text "Short US English status. No secrets. Name SHA / evidence path."
```

`-To` accepts `Phil`, `Robert`, or `Both`.

One-off without the helper (still read URL from the secrets file — do not
hardcode webhooks into commits or chat logs when avoidable):

```powershell
$hooks = Get-Content c:\analog-pim\.cursor\secrets\slack-webhooks.json -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json
$body = @{ text = "Your message" } | ConvertTo-Json
Invoke-RestMethod -Uri $hooks.phil -Method Post -ContentType "application/json" -Body $body
```

Expect Slack response body `ok`. On failure: log once, leave a draft, continue
the slice — do not retry-spam the webhook.

## Message content

- Plain US English; short; name evidence path / SHA when claiming done.
- No passwords, tokens, webhook URLs, Admin-Token, SoftHSM PINs, or other secrets.
- Honesty: Live / Partial / Absent / BLOCKED — never Met / certified from chrome.
- Webhooks post as the Slack app into a pre-chosen channel/DM destination;
  they are not a full Slack API (no user search, no thread reply unless the
  webhook destination already is that DM/channel).

## Forbidden constructs

- “Slack blocked / no Slack MCP / cannot notify” when
  `slack-webhooks.json` exists and has the needed key.
- Asking the operator to enable Slack MCP as the **only** path for a
  notification that webhooks can deliver.
- Committing webhook URLs to any repo, rule body, evidence README, or SPA.
- Pasting webhook URLs into partner-facing docs or Assessment Binder text.
- Using Slack to leak lab SOP into product UI (see
  [`no-lab-config-in-operator-ui.mdc`](no-lab-config-in-operator-ui.mdc)).

## MCP vs webhook

| Path | Use when |
| --- | --- |
| Slack MCP (`plugin-slack-slack` etc.) | Connected and tools work — DMs by user id, search, richer API |
| Incoming Webhooks | MCP missing or broken — **default fallback; agents are unblocked** |

